Confirmed the full chain for opa_vnic:

- Remote peer (InfiniBand fabric manager / any SMP-capable MAD sender) sends a vendor MAD with method GET/SET and attr OPA_EM_ATTR_IFACE_UCAST_MACS or OPA_EM_ATTR_IFACE_MCAST_MACS.
- `vema_recv` (line ~617) builds response MAD of size IB_MGMT_VENDOR_HDR + OPA_VNIC_EMA_DATA (data buffer = 2016 bytes on OPA? Let me verify OPA_MGMT_MAD_SIZE).
- `vema_get_mac_list` (opa_vnic_vema.c:455-479): `macs_out->num_macs_in_msg = macs_in->num_macs_in_msg;` — copies request field verbatim when nonzero, no clamp; only when zero uses max_entries = (OPA_VNIC_EMA_DATA - sizeof(*macs_out)) / ETH_ALEN.
- `opa_vnic_query_mcast_macs`/`ucast_macs` (opa_vnic_vema_iface.c:321/353): `num_macs = be16_to_cpu(macs->num_macs_in_msg)` then loops over netdev addr lists writing `macs->entry[count++]` — count bounded by num_macs (attacker-set up to 65535) AND by actual number of addresses in the list. Hmm wait — the writes are bounded by the number of actual MAC addresses in the adapter's netdev lists! count only increments when a ha matches. So the OOB write requires the adapter netdev to have more unicast/multicast MAC addresses than fit in the response. How many can a netdev have? netdev uc list is limited by netdev->uc.count vs max (addr_list_max). For a vnic netdev... default addr_list_max? netdev uc list max is typically NETDEV_MAX_UC? Actually `netdev_uc_count` bounded by `uc->max` which defaults to `netdev->uc.count > netdev->addr_list_max`? Let me check: default addr_list_max is -1 (unlimited) for most devices. Multicast list is essentially unlimited (up to memory). So an attacker controlling the netdev's multicast list — wait, can they? The netdev here is the opa_vnic ethernet device inside the host. An unprivileged local user could join many multicast addresses on that interface via socket options (IP_ADD_MEMBERSHIP etc.), growing netdev->mc list. Then the remote EM sends a GET IFACE_MCAST_MACS MAD with num_macs_in_msg = 0xFFFF, and query_mcast_macs writes entry[count] for every mc address, up to num_macs = 65535 entries × ETH_ALEN(6) bytes = 393210 bytes into rsp_mad->data[2016] — massive heap overflow.

So: the attacker = remote IB peer (fabric manager) supplying the count; the "many MACs" precondition is locally achievable. Even without local help, if the netdev has N macs, response writes N*6 bytes; N > 336 entries overflows. Actually, even num_macs=0 default max_entries=336 is safe (fits 2016-8-... wait sizeof(*macs_out)=8, (2016-8)/6=334). If request count is nonzero, the loop breaks when count == num_macs, but count only reaches the number of real addresses. So the write bound = min(num_macs, #addresses). Overflow needs #addresses > 334. With multicast, a local unprivileged user can add thousands of multicast addresses on any interface? Actually setting multicast on an interface requires... netlink or setsockopt IP_ADD_MEMBERSHIP requires no privilege for multicast joins. Yes, any local user can join multicast groups (bounded by sysctl igmp_max_memberships=20 per socket by default, but many sockets → many addresses; also the interface must be an ethernet device here — opa_vnic netdev — multicast join via raw packet socket or IPv4 on the opa_vnic interface... the opa_vnic netdev is a real ethernet device usable by local users).

This is a genuine remote-triggered heap overflow: remote IB peer controls the count copied verbatim into the response, response buffer sized only for the default max; the loop writes #addrs entries. This matches the "unclamped wire count" branch: count copied from wire into shared object with no range check; the sibling/default path (count==0) enforces max_entries but the nonzero mirror path does not — exactly the asymmetric-validation pattern.

