Precondition: adapter->netdev must have >334 unicast/multicast addresses. Unicast list on a netdev: addr_list_max default? In register_netdevice / dev_uc_init: `uc->max = -1` (unlimited) unless driver sets priv_flags. For multicast: mc list unlimited. So local user with access to the opa_vnic netdev can grow mc list arbitrarily (each IP_ADD_MEMBERSHIP adds one). Actually for multicast, the kernel adds the address via ip_mc_inc_group → __dev_mc_add — yes. sysctl igmp_max_memberships limits per-socket but multiple sockets or SIOCADDMULTI via raw socket... raw socket can call SIOCADDMULTI? Requires CAP_NET_ADMIN? ip_mc_join_group... For unprivileged: joining multicast groups is allowed (igmp_max_memberships=20 default, but 20 sockets × 20 groups = 400 > 334). Also there may already be many MACs in a real deployment (VNIC with lots of configured addresses — actually the ucast list of the opa vnic includes the EM-specified base MAC and any locally added). Even without local user, a malicious EM (the peer) is the one who sets the VESWPORT_MAC_ENTRIES... those go to the mac_tbl hash, not netdev uc list. Hmm, but the mc list can be grown by any local user via sockets; also the remote attacker could potentially grow the mc list by other means (e.g., the veswport info set includes... no).

